# Git for Windows turns LF into CRLF on checkout by default, and three kinds of file in this
# repository stop working when it does. Nothing in CI can see any of it: the runners are Linux,
# so the conversion never happens there.
#
# A shell script read by bash gets a \r on the end of every line, so `set -euo pipefail` is read
# as an option called `pipefail\r`. That one hides its own cause, because the carriage return
# makes the message overwrite itself - what reaches the terminal is
# `: invalid option nameine 18: set: pipefail`, which names neither the file nor the reason.
# `scripts/secrets.sh` could not run on Windows from the day it was added.
#
# `.husky/pre-commit` is one line, `pnpm exec lint-staged`, and sh looks for a command called
# `lint-staged\r`. So the hook that formats a commit fails on every Windows commit.
#
# And prettier's `endOfLine` default is `lf`, so `pnpm lint` rejects every file in a Windows
# checkout while passing in CI - the kind of red that teaches people to stop reading the check.
#
# So: LF in the working tree on every platform. Git already stores LF in the index, so this
# changes no file's content, only what a checkout writes. Binary files are detected by
# `text=auto` and left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one file whose target platform is Windows: the agent's installer runs on the school's office
# computer, under PowerShell 5.1. It reads LF fine, but this is the only script here that nobody
# on this team executes, so a doubt about it is cheaper to remove than to carry. Prettier does not
# format .ps1, so nothing else disagrees.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Bytes, never converted. `text=auto` already detects these; saying so is for the patch, which
# pnpm applies byte for byte, and the fonts, where a corruption is found at the first invoice PDF
# rather than at the commit that caused it.
*.patch -text
*.ttf -text
